{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1560254-mythological-origins-for-the-dur-abzu-and-what-the-hell-is-amma-su","id":1560254,"title":"Mythological Origins for the Dur-Abzu, and what the hell is Amma Su","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-05-29T05:56:10-07:00","id":1560254,"title":"Mythological Origins for the Dur-Abzu, and what the hell is Amma Su","content":"Nameless and Accursed is probably my favourite supplement for Mage, and largely that\u2019s because its the first time i felt like Scelesti were portrayed in a way that didn't feel too one-note (their depictions as utter Nihilists) or solely morally depraved (see Blood of the lamb) to write characters for. The many Dur-Abzu give a fun toolbox to invent yantras, Nimbus', magical styles and legacies. it added a framework to do Abyssal magic without relying on entities that would probably kill you or just making it &quot;This is a legacy, but watch out its EVIL &gt;:3&quot; all while not making it safe in any sense of the word. I know alot of the terms and ideas actually originated in left hand path but i thought the mechanics for antinomian magic in that book kinda sucked so i'm going to ignore it.<br /> <br /> Which brings me to the Dur-Abzus themselves which are full of interesting symbolism. Firstly is the word &quot;Dur-Abzu&quot; itself, the prefix &quot;Dur&quot; is an Akkadian word meaning &quot;Fortress of&quot; whereas &quot;Abzu&quot; refers to the primordial freshwater ocean beneath and above the world, Dark Eras states that ancient mages referred to the Abyss as the Abzu. so a Dur-Abzu is a Fortress of the Abyss. Anti-Watchtowers are referred to as &quot;Ziggurats&quot; a type of terraced building used to house temples in ancient Mesopotamia, Along with the terms &quot;Rabashakim&quot; and &quot;Baal&quot; Scelesti have a unique vocabulary owing to the origins of organised antinomian magic in ancient Babylon, this section of Mage lore was first established i think in &quot;Left-Hand path&quot; but i could be wrong about that. &quot;Dark Eras&quot; touches on it and &quot;Tome of the pentacle&quot; states that the city of Kish was ruled by a group of Scelesti before the first alliance of disparate mages formed to stop them, a conflict that resulted in the great flood of Mesopotamia.<br /> <br /> <b>Ao Si, the Mound of Thorns</b>: chosen probably because of the amount of British folklore associated with the Acanthus path &quot; Aos S\u00ed&quot; rather than a location actually refers to a race of elf like beings who live in &quot;Si&quot;, giant burial mounds. These elves were said to live in burial mounds and much of Irish folklore revolves around appeasing or not offending the &quot;fair folk&quot;. Weirdly enough the Ao Si don't seem actively malevolent but they may have been chosen for Scelesti symbols because they were later thought of as fallen angels not wicked enough for hell or (more likely) the writers of left hand path couldn't find anything else with the right aesthetics. in light of this the &quot;Mound&quot; of thorns is more akin to a tomb (where all fates end) made of corrupted Arcadian thorns thus representing the Lie of determinism and inevitability.<br /> <br /> <b>Drugaskan, the Impenetrable Darkness</b>: its not easy to dig up accurate information on Zoroastrianism but my best guess is that Drugaskan translates to something like &quot;love of deception&quot; or &quot;a lover of deception&quot; in persian and is the deepest part in Duzakh, the pit serving as the hell of Persian mythology, housing Ahriman and his demonds. the Impenetrable Darkness refers to the darkness of Angra Mainyu, in contrast to the virtuous light of Ahura Mazda. The darkness symbolises the cosmic forces of evil and corruption Joined Mastigos are thus deceived into embracing the Lie of isolation and damnation. I think that from a writing perspective, whilst Drugaskan is pretty fucking metal, the description of its landscape as just &quot;its dark lol&quot; makes it hard to write Joinings for characters, i like to detail HOW scelesti embrace the Abyss when they become Nasnasi and am currently trying to come up with mechanics for it, but it requires some elbow grease to make the story not read in a really repetitive manner when in Drugaskan. But i am probably one of like... 3 people in the world who has this issue so its a real old man yells at cloud situation.<br /> <br /> <b>Arallu, the cave of rot and tarnish</b>: one of the easier names to research, Arallu refers to the underworld of Mesopotamian mythology (can scelesti leave the fertile crescent alone for five fucking minutes???) and chosen, im guessing, because unlike comparable underworlds like Hades there is no moral judgement within Arallu. All of the dead regardless of deeds or misdeeds suffer the same fate of just kinda wandering around a cave and being generally miserable, i would be miserable too if i had &quot;only dust for food&quot;. apparently the families of the deceased would pour them libations and make offerings of food so that the dead could have something to eat and drink though i doubt souls trapped in the Abyss get such a privilege (could you encounter trapped souls or damned supernal entities during a Joining? food for thought(hah, get it?)) also its supposed to be full of demons, I'm unsure if the demons torture people or if they just kinda hang out. the Cave of Rot and Tarnish, is likely just an epithet describing the heart of Arallu, a landscape illustrating the Lie of materialism and stagnation. I initially didn't like this Dur-Abzu because i felt it wasn't &quot;distinct&quot; enough from the regular moros path symbols but i eventually came round to it when one of my players made a Nasnasi Moros. They used a broken rolex as a dedicated path tool and used at various times, blood diamonds, vinegarised wine and a blunted bronze sword.<br /> <br /> <b>Tartarus, the Deluge</b>: by far the easiest of the lot to research, Tartarus is the Hell of Greek and later Roman mythology, said to be &quot;as far beneath Hades as Heaven is above the earth&quot; Tartarus is a deep and dark pit where the wicked dead are punished for eternity (or for a finite duration depends on the myth). it also serves as the prison of monsters, once the Cyclops and the Hecatoncheires\u200b, but now the titans and whoever last pissed off an Olympian. Tartarus was probably chosen because of aesthetic reasons with so many Greek terms in Mage but it may be in reference to the Titanomachy\u200b, a war between the gods and titans that did horrendous damage to the universe. personally i think &quot;Ragnar\u00f6k&quot; would have been a better name for the Dur-Abzu but Norse imagery is sparse in Mage so it may have felt out of place. The Deluge is probably familiar to most of us westerners from the book of genesis. A planet spanning flood that wipes out the human race (only ten generations after Adam, that's like 2000 people max was a global flood necessary???) save for some guy in a boat. Flood myths are found in many cultures around the world, including (drum roll please) ancient Mesopotamia. The Deluge represents the forces of nature and supernature turned to catastrophic ends, enforcing the Lie of powerlessness before the forces of the universe.<br /> <br /> <b>Amma Su, the Burning Iron Spiral</b>: drawing from the mythology of who knows where representing God knows what, i haven't the foggiest idea where the word Amma Su originates. from what little i could put together &quot;Amma&quot; is a Tamil word meaning &quot;mother&quot; and &quot;Su&quot; means &quot;good&quot; or &quot;benevolent&quot;. but Su is used as a prefix not a suffix and &quot;Benevolent Mother&quot;??? for &quot;a continent-sized city of sprawling factories that belch black smoke into smog-filled air&quot;??? HUH???? my confusion probably stems from a lack of education in Hindu or Jain (I\u2019m guesstimating here) beliefs but i can't for the life of me figure out where the writers got the name for the Thyrsus Dur-Abzu, maybe it's a joke who knows. The Burning Iron Spiral has more obvious symbolism, bringing to mind images of burning fuels and industrial wastelands. i think the &quot;spiral&quot; is supposed to represent a sort of unified spirituality the Ecstatiscs and Shamans are known for, a shape that draws everything in and expands ever outwards, but its been twisted to represent the endless appetite of human civilisation. i've always imagined the spiral as a cone-like shape pointing upwards, rather than a two dimensional thing, Dunno why.<br /> <br /> for the record i am not a scholar and i do not speak the language of any culture from which these myths originated so please do correct any inaccuracies. but more importantly can anyone PLEASE TELL ME WHAT AMMA SU IS","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28510-communist-owl","name":"Communist Owl","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28510_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-31T05:40:07-07:00","id":1560284,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Ao Si, the Mound of Thorns: chosen probably because of the amount of British folklore associated with the Acanthus path &quot; Aos S\u00ed&quot; rather than a location actually refers to a race of elf like beings who live in &quot;Si&quot;, giant burial mounds. These elves were said to live in burial mounds and much of Irish folklore revolves around appeasing or not offending the &quot;fair folk&quot;. Weirdly enough the Ao Si don't seem actively malevolent but they may have been chosen for Scelesti symbols because they were later thought of as fallen angels not wicked enough for hell or (more likely) the writers of left hand path couldn't find anything else with the right aesthetics. in light of this the &quot;Mound&quot; of thorns is more akin to a tomb (where all fates end) made of corrupted Arcadian thorns thus representing the Lie of determinism and inevitability. </div> </div> </div>Na Daoine S\u00ed, or the people of the mounds in Irish, fit in this weird spot. Said to be the descendants of the Tuath D\u00e9 Danann (I've seen a few diffferent translations of this phrase, but the Tuath (clann?) of the Goddess Danann/Dan\u00fa seems reasonable) they were clearly men in the original tellings, or perhaps gods (plus a load of the stories were written down in the ~11th century by Christian monks, prefaced with &quot;now I don't believe this, but this is what people say...&quot; And fabricated genealogies tracing all the various people to a descendants of Noah... Or people's enslaved in Greece ... Either way, it doesn't feel like modern depictions of Fairies to me.<br /> <br /> They seem to have more in common with Heracles than with Tinkerbell.<br /> <br /> Still, the later depictions (at least from what I've seen) does have people marrying off and going to the land of the forever young. Which seems to be still very human. <br /> <br /> They are definitely not malevolent - fort example, the Bean S\u00ed (woman S\u00ed) more commonly spelt Banshee in English is a keening woman who warns of a coming death (with each banshee warning only a specific family?) has been blamed for causing the deaths rather than bring a protector spirits of sorts...<br /> <br /> But I don't think the Abyss is malevolent either. Like i see it as just wanting to exist (which is still a problem when it's existence is incompatible with reality), but not intrinsicly evil... <br /> <br /> I tend to dismiss the categorization of &quot;Fallen angels&quot; because (like the descendants of Noah source) it seems like a post-hoc addition to fit with the biblical narrative. But the comment is fine.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure how much Fate or Destiny is tied up with Na Daoine, any more so than it is with any story. But also i think they have a pretty broad cultural footprint, from Leprechauns to Banshees and modern politicians blaming the fairies for potholes... (You'll never guess whose family owns a company getting contracts to fix the roads!) I'm not aswell versed in this area as I would like. But tricksters more than serious existential threats seems more in line with them. Still not exactly Malevolent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-05-31T12:28:46-07:00","id":1560290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Communist Owl</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1560254-mythological-origins-for-the-dur-abzu-and-what-the-hell-is-amma-su#post1560254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Tartarus was probably chosen because of aesthetic reasons with so many Greek terms in Mage but it may be in reference to the Titanomachy, a war between the gods and titans that did horrendous damage to the universe.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's also a hell where a bunch of divinities were banished. Like, the Obrimos are all about heavens and divine power, so an invertion of that might be a realm of banishment for gods.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure how related it is, but the Classical Greek mages in <i>To The Strongest</i> also use a version of this metaphor. The 'Olympian' gods live in the Supernal whilst the 'titans' are the old gods who live in the Abyss and were pushed out by the Olympians.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Deluge represents the forces of nature and supernature turned to catastrophic ends, enforcing the Lie of powerlessness before the forces of the universe. </div> </div> </div>I take it a part of the symbolism is also that this deluge doesn't end. It's a divinity that destroys the world and does not relent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-10T19:48:34-07:00","id":1560541,"title":"","content":"I also like how the Dur-Abzu are inversions of the conventional/typical understandings of the Watchtowers. Especially since I do belive it's possible for a mages personal interpretation of the truths presented by theor awakening could align more with the atypical perceptions of the Dur-Abzu. <br /> <br /> I made an Acanthus who views things under a similar lens as Ao Si, accepts she sees no reason or excuse to nihilistic or malicious about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24310-metrere-oil","name":"Metrere Oil","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}